          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Bluetti Lineup Explained: Elite vs AC vs EB vs Apex
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bluetti sells more than a dozen power stations at any given time, and the model names do not make the differences obvious. The easiest way to read the catalog is to sort it into four families, each built for a different job. Once you know which family you belong in, choosing a specific model usually comes down to capacity and budget.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Elite series: the newest portable line</h3>

          <p>
            The Elite V2 models are Bluetti's current generation of portable power stations, and they are where most of the brand's engineering effort has gone over the past two years. The Elite 30 V2, Elite 100 V2 and Elite 200 V2 share a common design language: lighter shells than the older AC models, LiFePO4 batteries with high cycle ratings, fast UPS switchover and higher solar input for their size. The trade-off is that Elite models are fixed-capacity. None of the three accepts an expansion battery, so you are buying a finished product rather than the start of a system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">AC series: the workhorse portables</h3>

          <p>
            The AC line is older and broader. The AC180 and AC70 are fixed-capacity portables that predate the Elite V2 refresh and are now often discounted. The AC200L is the expandable member of the family, accepting B230, B300 and B300K batteries. Above those sit the AC300 and AC500, modular units with no internal battery that pair with B300 or B300S packs and can be linked for 240V. They still work well, but Bluetti now points home-backup buyers toward the Apex 300 instead.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">EB series: the entry level</h3>

          <p>
            The EB models are the small, inexpensive end of the catalog. The EB3A is the one still worth buying, a 268Wh, 600W box that frequently sells for less than any other LiFePO4 unit Bluetti makes. EB stands, informally, for entry battery, and that is the right way to think of it: a device charger and CPAP backup, not an outage plan for a whole kitchen.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Apex and EP: home energy systems</h3>

          <p>
            The Apex 300 and the EP-series units are built to back up a house rather than a campsite. The Apex 300 is still technically portable on wheels, but it is designed around expansion batteries, home-panel integration and 240V output. The EP760 and EP900 go further, as wall- or floor-mounted systems that pair with B500 batteries and are installed by a professional alongside solar. If you are shopping in this tier, read our guide to the <a href="/best-home-battery-backup-system/">best home battery backup systems</a> too, because Bluetti is competing with Tesla, Enphase and EcoFlow here, not just with other portables.
          </p>
